---
// Custom Tolo.Cloud case study. Replaces the Notion-rendered version of
// the same slug. Notion still drives the homepage list metadata (title,
// tags, cover, order) so this page is wired into /#work the same way the
// other case studies are — only the body content is custom.
//
// Lifts the portal's dark palette directly, embeds live demos of the
// real BucketCard surface, the promote-to-Live pipeline, the card
// iteration arc, and the engine's verbs-as-contract surface.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import CSCodeBlock from "../../components/case-study/CSCodeBlock.astro";
import BucketCardShowcase from "../../components/case-study/showcase/BucketCardShowcase.astro";
import CardEvolutionScrubber from "../../components/case-study/showcase/CardEvolutionScrubber.astro";
import PromoteButtonDemo from "../../components/case-study/showcase/PromoteButtonDemo.astro";
import VerbsHub from "../../components/case-study/showcase/VerbsHub.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Tolo.Cloud — Jon Tollefson"
  description="A creative archive that doesn't try to be creative for me. Twelve UI rounds. Two architectural walls. One verbs-as-contract engine."
  ogImage="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · May 2026 — present"
    title="Tolo.Cloud"
    subtitle="A creative archive that doesn't try to be creative for me."
    cover="/case-studies/the-console/cover.jpg"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Designer, builder, sole engineer" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "May 2026 — present" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Astro · FastAPI · Python · Pedalboard · Claude Code" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · Engineer live 2026-05-22" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ───────── 01 · Setup ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The setup" deck="Years of audio, scattered across iCloud, T7, Voice Memos, Ableton bounces on three drives, a Splice library, the LiveTrak Recordings dir, a drop folder." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>No master list. No way to ask "what's interesting in here" without listening through hours of material. No path from a captured fragment to a clip in a Live project beyond drag, drop, trim, warp.</p>
      <p>The obvious response is to let the machine do it. Point a model at the archive and have it write the next track. That is the wrong response.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        Utility, not synthesis. The computer sorts, fingerprints, surfaces, and removes format friction. The artist still chooses, still drags, still arranges.
      </CSPullquote>

      <p>Tolo.Cloud was built on that principle and stayed there at every decision point. Friction gets removed at the catalog level and the format-conformance level, not at the level of creative intent.</p>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 20279, suffix: "", label: "rows in inventory.db", color: "white" },
        { n: 99.7, suffix: "%", format: "decimal", label: "waveform coverage", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 215, label: "promote-ready loops", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 6, label: "variants per mix job", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 02 · The card ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="The thing you touch" deck="Everything else is in service of this row. Click into the title to rename it. Click the waveform to play. Hold the ✕ on the right to archive. Click a category to bucket it.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · TRY IT"
      caption="The actual BucketCard. Real interactions wired up to mock state — rename, bucket, expand, audio sweep, hold-to-archive. Multiple state variants below show how it looks across the catalog."
      bleed
    >
      <BucketCardShowcase
        title="NOIZU_125_shaker_loop_tight_01"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:04"
        bpm={99}
        keyStr="E major"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "06-15-2024", days: "704d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        tags={["loop", "sample", "drum"]}
        contentType="music-only"
        sourceType="splice"
        expanded
      />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The catalog has 20,279 rows after a one-time backfill across four iCloud roots, the T7 drive, and two Photos library packages. 8,395 of those are user-origin; the rest is stock content the sweeper learned to filter out. Each row gets a waveform PNG, a friendly display name, BPM and key when audible, a content-type classification, and a soft-delete shadow on archive.</p>
      <p>The card is dense on purpose. The title is the identity. The waveform is the play surface. The category pill is the only commitment the curator makes per row. Everything else expands on demand.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-grid-2">
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-voice"
        title="VOX_Tolo_0007"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:30"
        date={{ rel: "1y", stamp: "10-23-2024", days: "574d" }}
        silenceRatio={0.42}
        bucket="field"
        contentType="voice-note"
        sourceType="voice_memo"
        tags={["idea-fragment", "first-person"]}
      />
      <BucketCardShowcase
        cardId="bc-inlive"
        title="55_Cmaj_0_what_you_know"
        ext=".wav"
        duration="0:08"
        bpm={55}
        keyStr="C major"
        date={{ rel: "5d", stamp: "05-17-2026", days: "5d" }}
        bucket="loops"
        inLive
        contentType="loop"
        tags={["loop", "sample"]}
        expanded
      />
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 03 · The wall ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="The wall that bent the build" deck="Build 3 was a Max-for-Live device living inside Ableton. Two architectural walls killed it. The reframe is the spine of the whole project.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The plan was a device inside Ableton: select a row in the catalog, drag the audio onto a clip slot in the Live set, have it warp and snap. The device was code-complete in <code>~/console-build/device/</code> (preserved on M3 as historical record) when two architectural walls killed the direction.</p>

      <p><strong>The first wall.</strong> <code>jweb</code> (the Max object that hosts a webview inside a Max patch) does not propagate drag events out to the surrounding Live UI. You can render a beautiful list of catalog rows inside the device. You cannot drag one of those rows into a clip slot. The drag dies at the jweb boundary.</p>

      <p><strong>The second wall.</strong> <code>ClipSlot.create_clip</code> in Live's LiveAPI is MIDI-only. There is no audio equivalent. You cannot programmatically bring an audio clip into existence at a path on disk. You can fire an existing clip, mute it, unmute it, set warp markers on a clip that already exists. Creating one from scratch with an audio source: not exposed.</p>

      <CSPullquote>
        Stop building a synthetic surface inside the DAW. Use the DAW's existing one.
      </CSPullquote>

      <p>Ableton's Browser already lets you drag audio into a clip slot from any Place you've registered. So make <code>_Soundbending/loops/</code> a Place. Make sure every promoted loop lands there normalized, named with BPM and key in the filename, ready to drag. The device disappears. The DAW's own UI does the work the device was supposed to do. None of this required new code inside Live. The promote pipeline writes a file. Live picks it up.</p>
    </div>

    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK PROMOTE"
      caption="The Buckets-to-Live bridge in one click. Copy → loudnorm → set live_loop_path. The button flips to ✓ IN LIVE the moment the engine's done. Re-runs are idempotent."
    >
      <PromoteButtonDemo />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The first three test loops landed in <code>_Soundbending/loops/</code> end-to-end on the evening of 2026-05-17. The Phase 2 step (generating a sibling <code>.alc</code> Ableton Live Clip file so loop region and warp markers are pre-locked) was deliberately deferred. The <code>.wav</code>-only version was already enough to ship the unlock; the <code>.alc</code> format is a gzipped XML wrapper better reverse-engineered from a real saved clip than guessed at programmatically.</p>
      <p>The catalog became the source of truth. The DAW's browser became the surface. The artist's gesture became the bridge.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 04 · The card grew up ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="The card grew up" deck="Twelve visible rounds of design between 2026-05-18 and 2026-05-21. Each round was a subtraction or a reframe. Drag the slider to scrub through history.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · DRAG TO SCRUB"
      caption="One card markup, six designed eras applied via a single class swap. Each round is a real git commit. The brand emerged through restraint, not addition."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <CardEvolutionScrubber />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The design conventions that survived: title in italic mono 17px on a green-wash chip. BPM and key as a borderless italic mono subtitle, number and letter in cyan <code>#0ec4fc</code>, unit and mode in muted cream. LCD duration in pink, left of the waveform. The waveform doubles as the play surface. J/L/F/T pill as a vertical medicine container with one active tile.</p>
      <p>What got cut, in order: star-rating buttons, ⚡ kudos boosts, the ⚡ ticker, per-tile borders on the pill, the dedicated ▶ button, the X keyboard shortcut, the +Queue button, the keyboard-shortcut help panel, the sub-tab strip, the Surprise tab, the Library tab.</p>
      <p>The brand rename arrived in the middle of this. "The Console" became "Tolo.Cloud" on 2026-05-19. The hidden trigger word on the public home page changed from <code>console</code> to <code>cloud</code> the next day. The design had found itself; the wordmark caught up.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 05 · Three decisions ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="Three decisions, and what got cut" deck="The signal that lied was the model's own confidence value. The signal that worked was a per-file ratio anyone could have computed without a model.">

    <h3 class="cs-subhead">Density gate over <code>no_speech_prob</code></h3>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The classifier path is <code>mlx-whisper</code> for transcription on the M1, then Claude (via <code>claude -p</code> against the Max subscription keychain) for classification. The cost model assumed Whisper's own <code>no_speech_prob</code> would short-circuit instrumental music before Claude ever saw the transcript. It did not. The model confidently transcribes a synth melody into hallucinated lyrics, hands the result to Claude, and Claude either generates a summary of nonsense or correctly notes "Insufficient content." Either way, the call is billed.</p>
    </div>

    <CSCodeBlock file="~/automation/transcription/transcribe.py" lang="py" caption="Three-signal gate. avg_logprob was the reliable one — music hallucinations come back around -1.24, real voice around -0.5. The model's own no_speech_prob value was the unreliable one.">{`def looks_like_non_speech(seg: dict, duration_s: float) -> bool:
    """Return True when this segment is probably not speech.

    Whisper's own no_speech_prob is unreliable on clean instrumentals
    (returns near zero on synth melodies it then hallucinates lyrics
    over). avg_logprob is the signal that actually separates music from
    voice in production runs.
    """
    if seg.get("no_speech_prob", 0.0) >= 0.6:
        return True
    if seg.get("avg_logprob", 0.0) <= -1.0:
        return True

    text = (seg.get("text") or "").strip()
    if duration_s >= 60 and len(text) / max(duration_s, 1) < 2.0:
        # On long files, < 2 chars/sec of "transcript" is sparse content.
        # Normal speech runs 10-15 chars/sec.
        return True
    return False`}</CSCodeBlock>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The retry batch was 314 rows tagged "Insufficient content." from the original whisper pass. First retry without the density gate routed five M3 samples back to Claude, all returned "Insufficient content." again. The batch was aborted and the original summaries restored from a backup table I had created exactly because I expected to need it. Second retry with the density gate active flipped 97% of the rows to <code>music-only</code> server-side, zero Claude calls on the gated rows.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="cs-subhead"><code>claude -p</code> over Claude API credits</h3>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Two paid resources were always in play: a Claude API key with metered credits, and a Max subscription with a logged-in <code>claude</code> CLI. The classifier dispatcher chose between three paths in order: <code>claude_code</code> (the CLI against the local macOS keychain) when <code>CLASSIFIER=claude_code</code> is set, then Claude API, then Ollama as a local fallback. Production runs on the keychain path. Roughly 5× faster than Ollama at classify (~7 seconds vs ~30 per row). No API credits burned.</p>
      <p>The constraint was platform: SSH-spawned <code>claude -p</code> cannot access the login keychain (Apple architectural limit on TCC inheritance for ssh sessions). Only launchd-spawned subprocesses inherit keychain access. So the engine's <code>POST /classify</code> endpoint became a proxy. M3 worker hits the endpoint over Tailscale. The launchd-spawned engine on M1 shells out to <code>claude -p</code> and returns the classification. Same model, free under the Max subscription, no SSH path involved.</p>
    </div>

    <h3 class="cs-subhead">Forward-looking Print Capture over retroactive bounce detection</h3>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The catalog knew about 212 Ableton <code>.als</code> projects after the backfill. The bounced-track auto-detect heuristic walked each project folder for a <code>Bounces/</code> or <code>Renders/</code> subdirectory containing a 2-track audio file matching the project name. It found zero. Bounces scatter — they land on the Desktop, get attached to email, get dropped into a friend's Dropbox, get sent to a mastering chain that lives somewhere else entirely.</p>
      <p>The fix was to stop scanning and start watching. An M3-side daemon (<code>com.tolo.prints-watcher</code>) polls <code>~/.../_Soundbending/prints/inbox/</code> every 60 seconds. New stable <code>.wav</code> files get POSTed to the M1 engine's <code>/prints/notice</code> endpoint. When the curator visits <code>/portal/projects</code> later, a banner shows the pending print with the top three <code>.als</code> suggestions ranked by mtime proximity. One click links.</p>
      <p>Lineage runs both directions after the link. Forward: each project page lists <code>linked_prints</code>. Reverse: a project that samples a print elsewhere shows a <code>built_from_prints</code> chip with the multi-hop chain. Mastering passes naturally produce multi-hop chains because each pass writes a new print that the next session samples from. The catalog learned to read the production graph instead of guessing about it.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 06 · Verbs are the contract ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="Verbs are the contract" deck="The whole system is a FastAPI process. Each endpoint is a verb. Click any verb to see its real shape.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · CLICK A VERB"
      caption="The actual engine surface running on M1. When the Surprise and Library tabs got deleted on 2026-05-17, the catalog didn't change. Three surfaces collapsed into one. That's what verbs-as-contract buys."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <VerbsHub />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <CSPullquote cite="from the session log that triggered the 3-tab consolidation">
      "i think at this point it'd make sense to consolidate the console's ui. library doesn't make sense anymore as a separate page, the bucket's page is really the library now... 'surprise' page also feels like bloat now."
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 07 · The Engineer (preview) ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="The Engineer" deck="Build 5. A per-track mix agent that renders six variants across three taste axes and presents them as A/B pairs. Audio tinder.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Once the catalog could promote loops into Live, the next question was: can the same engine help me <em>finish</em> the work, not by writing it, but by giving me a fast taste-feedback loop on mixing decisions? Build 5 is that. Three taste axes (focal, space, dynamics), six variants per job, A/B pair-tinder UI with free-text per-pair comments and cross-axis memory.</p>
      <p>The pipeline is stems-first. Section detection runs in the analyzer via librosa recurrence-matrix + agglomerative segmentation; the planner is <code>claude -p</code> against the M1 keychain, told that the source defines the focal element, the masking conflicts, the stem relationships. Each variant gets rendered with pedalboard. After every render, five Pedalboard chains simulate phone / AirPods / laptop / car / club playback. Per-band energy deltas and stereo collapse for mono targets translate to plain-language flags.</p>
      <p>Engineer shipped to production via Vercel on 2026-05-22 morning. Phase 5 (reference catalog of artists I love, pulled as taste anchors into the planner's prompt context) is in design.</p>
    </div>

    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/the-console/diagram-engineer-flow.svg"
      alt="The Engineer pipeline: source stems → librosa analyzer with section detection → claude planner reasoning per stem per section → pedalboard renderer → 5-target translator. Below: three taste axes and an A/B pair card with reasoning, translation badges, solo bar."
      caption="Tier B will replace this static diagram with a live A/B pair card you can switch sides on, with mock audio playback and visible reasoning."
      width="bleed"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 08 · The boundary ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="08" title="What it deliberately does not do" deck="The won't-do list is short on purpose and gets checked before any feature lands.">
    <ul class="wont">
      <li>Write Substack, LinkedIn, or Instagram posts without an explicit draft invocation.</li>
      <li>Propose albums, EPs, or song concepts.</li>
      <li>Push daily prompts.</li>
      <li>Listen to voice commands during a session.</li>
      <li>Touch the desktop.</li>
      <li>Publish to streaming platforms.</li>
      <li>Make creative decisions.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Every one of those is a feature an adjacent product ships. Every one is a place where the machine pretends to author. The cost of including any of them is the same: I stop trusting that the surface is mine. A catalog that emails me an unsolicited "draft album concept" stops being a catalog and becomes a parasocial collaborator. That trade was off the table from the start, and the build kept it off the table at every decision point.</p>
      <p>Whisper transcribes. Claude classifies. The Engineer renders variants and reasons about them. None of them synthesize. The synthesis happens later, in Live, by hand.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ───────── 09 · Where this points ───────── */}
  <CSSection num="09" title="Where this points" deck="The point of the build is not the build. The point is more music made.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p><code>uxjon.com/sounds</code> already exists as a public route. Items flip to public via a toggle on any card. The infrastructure for the future state (a public-facing surface of finished work that streams from the same engine that catalogs the raw material) is already shipped. The path from "this loop is interesting" to "this track is out" runs through the same database and the same verbs.</p>
      <p>3,750 rows still sit in the inbox bucket. 215 promotable loops are queued. The machine just made choosing cheaper.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  /* Prose blocks left-align under the section head so the eye picks up
     the head and the first paragraph at the same x position. Width caps
     at 64ch for readability. */
  .prose-block {
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote {
    max-width: 64ch;
  }

  .cs-subhead {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 20px;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--brand-cyan);
    margin: 36px 0 12px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .cs-subhead code {
    font-family: inherit;
    font-style: italic;
    background: rgba(14, 196, 252, 0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(14, 196, 252, 0.18);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--brand-cyan);
  }

  .card-grid-2 {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 18px;
    margin: 24px 0 8px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 880px) {
    .card-grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  .wont {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    max-width: 64ch;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 6px 22px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .wont { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  .wont li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 22px;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.78);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wont li::before {
    content: "—";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--brand-pink);
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
